-- Q4962: SOC Report Content and Structure [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The geographic locations where the service organization maintains its data centers","The services, infrastructure, software, people, and data included in or excluded from the examination","The maximum number of user entities permitted to rely on the service auditor''s report","The specific time frame during which the service auditor conducted fieldwork procedures"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under SSAE 18 (AT-C 320), system boundaries define the scope of a SOC engagement by identifying which services, infrastructure, software, people, procedures, and data are included in or excluded from the examination. This scoping is essential for user entities to understand what the report covers. Wrong (A): Geographic locations may be disclosed in the system description, but boundaries define scope of services and components, not physical locations alone. Wrong (C): SOC reports do not limit the number of user entities that may rely on them; boundaries define what is examined, not who may use the report. Wrong (D): The examination period is a separate element; system boundaries define the components subject to examination, not the fieldwork timeline.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4962;

-- Q4963: SOC Report Content and Structure [hard/L4]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The auditor found no exceptions or deviations in any controls tested during the period","The description and controls are fairly presented and effective, except for specifically identified matters","The service organization''s controls are pervasively ineffective and cannot be relied upon","The auditor could not obtain sufficient evidence to form any opinion on the controls"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under SSAE 18 (AT-C 205), a qualified opinion in a SOC 2 Type II report indicates that, except for specific matters described in the opinion, the system description is fairly presented and controls are suitably designed and operating effectively. It signals a limited deficiency rather than a pervasive failure. Wrong (A): No exceptions or deviations describes an unqualified (clean) opinion, not a qualified one. Wrong (C): Pervasively ineffective controls would result in an adverse opinion, which is more severe than a qualification. Wrong (D): Inability to obtain sufficient evidence results in a disclaimer of opinion, not a qualified opinion.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4963;

-- Q4964: SOC Report Content and Structure [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The auditor must reject all management representations as inherently unreliable until proven otherwise","The auditor should seek the most persuasive evidence reasonably obtainable rather than accepting weaker alternatives","The auditor must assume fraud exists until sufficient evidence conclusively proves otherwise","The auditor should accept corroborating evidence only from sources external to the entity"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AU-C 200, presumptive doubt means the auditor should not be satisfied with less-than-persuasive evidence when more persuasive evidence could reasonably be obtained. Here, the partner correctly sought independent corroboration rather than relying solely on a management representation. Wrong (A): Presumptive doubt does not require rejecting all management representations; it requires seeking stronger evidence when available. Wrong (C): Presumptive doubt differs from an assumption of fraud — it demands rigorous evidence standards without presuming dishonesty. Wrong (D): While external evidence is often more persuasive, presumptive doubt does not categorically exclude internal sources.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4964;

-- Q4965: SOC Report Content and Structure [hard/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Relies exclusively on analytical procedures and disregards all substantive testing results","Selects sample items from only one population segment rather than the full population","Delegates all professional judgment to specialists without independent evaluation","Gives disproportionate weight to an initial data point and insufficiently adjusts for new information"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Anchoring bias occurs when an auditor over-relies on an initial reference point, such as a prior-year balance, and fails to sufficiently adjust expectations for current conditions (AU-C 520, Analytical Procedures). The senior''s expectation was anchored to last year''s revenue, underweighting the new customer''s impact. Wrong (A): Ignoring substantive testing describes an audit methodology error, not the cognitive bias of anchoring to initial information. Wrong (B): Selecting from one population segment describes a sampling limitation, not a cognitive bias related to over-reliance on initial data. Wrong (C): Delegating to specialists without evaluation relates to professional skepticism in using experts, not anchoring.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4965;

-- Q5798: Data Protection Technologies [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Preventing unauthorized key rotation performed by privileged administrators without approval","Allowing authorized decryption when the key holder is unavailable or during legal proceedings","Replacing the organization''s key management infrastructure with a simpler third-party solution","Enabling real-time revocation of compromised keys across all distributed systems simultaneously"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Per NIST SP 800-57 Part 1, key escrow deposits a copy of cryptographic keys with a trusted third party to ensure authorized access when the primary key holder is unavailable, such as during employee termination or legal discovery. This supports business continuity and regulatory compliance. Wrong (A): Key escrow addresses key availability, not the prevention of unauthorized key rotation, which is handled by access controls on key management systems. Wrong (C): Key escrow adds complexity to key management rather than eliminating it, since the escrow agent requires its own secure storage and access controls. Wrong (D): Key revocation is a separate key lifecycle function; escrow ensures recoverability, not revocation capability.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 5798;

-- Q5859: Data Protection Technologies [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["File-level encryption applied to selected sensitive documents only","Full disk encryption covering the entire storage volume transparently","Network-layer encryption protecting data during transmission between systems","Database-level encryption securing structured data stored within an application"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Per NIST SP 800-111, full disk encryption (FDE) encrypts the entire contents of a storage device, including the operating system, applications, and temporary files. FDE provides comprehensive volume-level protection without requiring users to select individual files for encryption. Wrong (A): File-level encryption protects only designated files and may leave temporary files, swap space, and OS data exposed on a lost device. Wrong (C): Network-layer encryption protects data in transit between systems, not data at rest on a laptop''s hard drive. Wrong (D): Database-level encryption secures structured data within a database engine, not the full disk contents including the OS and temp files.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 5859;

-- Q5881: Data Protection Technologies [medium/L3]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The archived documents become permanently inaccessible once the previous key is retired","The previous key must be retained in a secure archive to decrypt those historical documents","The archived documents automatically re-encrypt under the new key during the rotation process","The rotation has no impact because AES-256 encryption keys are inherently backward compatible"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Per NIST SP 800-57 Part 1, key rotation does not automatically re-encrypt previously encrypted data. Organizations must retain expired keys in a secure key archive to maintain access to historical data, which increases key lifecycle management complexity. Wrong (A): Documents do not become permanently inaccessible — the previous key can be retained in a secure archive for authorized decryption when needed. Wrong (C): Automatic re-encryption would require decrypting and re-encrypting all 2.3 million documents, a resource-intensive process that does not occur during standard rotation. Wrong (D): AES-256 keys are not backward compatible; each document remains encrypted with the specific key used at the time of encryption.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 5881;

-- Q5902: Data Protection Technologies [medium/L3]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["TDE significantly degrades query performance, making it unsuitable for production database workloads","TDE requires application code modifications to handle encryption and decryption during queries","TDE protects data at rest on disk but leaves data unencrypted in memory during query processing","TDE cannot encrypt databases containing more than ten million rows of customer records"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Per NIST SP 800-111, Transparent Data Encryption encrypts database files at the storage layer, protecting against physical media theft. However, data is decrypted in memory during query processing, leaving it vulnerable to memory-scraping attacks on the server. Wrong (A): TDE has minimal performance impact because encryption occurs at the I/O level and is designed for production workloads. Wrong (B): TDE operates transparently below the application layer — applications read and write data without code changes, which is its primary advantage. Wrong (D): TDE has no record count limitation; it encrypts the entire database regardless of size.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 5902;

